toDual -- finds the inverse system to an ideal up to a given degree
*******************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        M = toDual (ZZ, I)
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, in a standard graded
        polynomial ring
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * DividedPowers => ..., default value true
  * Outputs:
      * M, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, interpreted as list of
        generators of inverse system to I

Description
===========

If I is an ideal, then toDual(d,I) = inverseSystem(d, I). See the documentation
of InverseSystems for the basic theory and of inverseSystem for the function.

If I is a matrix, then the code first replaces it with the ideal generated by
the entries.

Caveat
======

The Base ring of the input must be a standard graded polynomial ring over a
field.

See also
========

  * *note InverseSystems: Top, -- equivariant Macaulay inverse systems
  * *note inverseSystem: inverseSystem, -- Inverse systems with equivariance
  * *note fromDual: fromDual, -- Ideal from inverse system

Ways to use toDual:
===================

  * "toDual(ZZ,Ideal)"
  * "toDual(ZZ,Matrix)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note toDual: toDual, is a *note method function with options:
(Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

